    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2024/06/asics-superblast-2-review-2024.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Max-stack cushioning (45/37 mm) gives strong impact protection and a highly cushioned underfoot feel, but it is tuned slightly firm rather than mushy, with a smooth heel bevel and snappy forefoot sensation.",
        "Responsiveness: The FF Turbo+ setup feels bouncier than v1 with clear energy return, and the rocker geometry adds snappiness that shines at uptempo and marathon efforts, though transitions start somewhat stiff before break-in.",
        "Stability: The platform is rigid with very little flex/torsion, and the moderately to very stiff padded heel counter plus secure upper lockdown provide solid lateral support with minimal foot translation.",
        "Durability: Durability is repeatedly described as excellent/above average, with reported zero outsole wear at 35 road miles and expectations of long material life, while outsole grip is best on roads/smooth surfaces rather than aggressive terrain."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2024/07/article-by-michael-ellenberger-asics.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The 45 mm heel / 37 mm forefoot max-stack setup provides strong impact protection and a highly cushioned heel/forefoot feel, though some runners may find it slightly firmer than v1.",
        "Responsiveness: The FF TURBO PLUS + FF BLAST PLUS ECO midsole is described as bouncy, energetic, and snappy, with stronger rebound as pace increases.",
        "Stability: Lateral support is strong from the wide platform (90/80/120 mm), sidewall sit-in feel, and improved upper lockdown; no explicit torsional rigidity or heel-counter stiffness test is reported.",
        "Durability: The outsole is noted as more durable than v1 with improved grip/traction (especially in wet conditions) and better long-term wear, including removal of gravel-catching cutouts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/asics-superblast-2-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The Superblast 2 feels slightly softer than v1 with a smooth, bouncy ride; its 45/37 mm stack provides strong impact protection without feeling overly squishy.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is improved versus v1 (more bounce and release), making it easy to get up to pace and hold it, but it is not especially snappy.",
        "Stability: The ride can feel a bit blocky, while the unchanged platform shape and secure lockdown are reported as stable with no major support issues.",
        "Durability: The AsicsGrip outsole is described as very tacky and an upgrade over AHAR with no traction issues so far, but long-term outsole/material wear is not yet established in the provided text."
      ]
    }
